<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.7. General Fourier series</title>

  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <definition xml:id="def-general-fourier-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function on the interval <m>[-L,L]</m>. Then the 
        <term>fourier series</term> for <m>f</m> on <m>[-L,L]</m> is 
        <md>
         \ds f(x) = A_0 + \sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty} \left[ A_n \cos\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)
         + B_n \sin\left(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\right)\right]
        </md>
        with coefficients <m>A_0</m> and <m>A_n, B_n</m> for <m>n=1, 2, \ldots</m> given by
        <md>
          <mrow>A_0 \amp = \frac{1}{2L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x) ~dx </mrow>
          <mrow>A_n \amp = \frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) ~dx </mrow>
          <mrow>B_n \amp = \frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) ~dx </mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-odd-even-functions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall <m>f(x)</m> is an <term>even function</term> if <m>f(x) = f(-x)</m>, and 
        <m>f(x)</m> is an <term>odd function</term> if <m>{f(x)=-f(-x)}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        First recall the Fourier sine and cosine series on <m>[0,L]</m> (not than the general Fourier series yet).
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the Fourier sine series for the function <m>f(x) = x</m> over
  the interval <m>[0,1]</m>.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now find the Fourier cosine series for this function <m>f(x) = x</m> over the same
  interval <m>[0,1]</m>.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

   <p>You should see the <term>Gibbs phenomenon</term>, an oscillating behavior at an endpoint,
     in one case but not the other.
  We'd like to understand why this happens.  </p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's look at another example.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the <em>general Fourier series</em> for the function <m>f(x) = x^2 + x
  + 1</m> over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use Mathematica (or your choice of technology) to graph the function <m>f(x)</m> and an approximation to
  the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.  No matter how many
  terms you add in the series approximation, your Fourier series should exhibit the <em>Gibbs
  phenomenon</em>, which shows a lot of oscillations near the endpoints.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now graph just the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-4,4]</m>.  Here
  you should see the periodicity in the Fourier series and the reason
  for the Gibbs phenomenon as the Fourier series is trying to track
  a discontinuity.</p>
        
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Stay tuned! We'll discuss this as a class in a little while.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
